{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D161-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d06970a2cc6eb334a2dbb18112be4118d0fd92fdc478dc9c4f402ac502e594d5\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 replaces finding a door with waking a wall; A5 replaces waking with listening, but B2 prompts no new mission.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2 with 'At doors' but repeats A1's mission; B4's nonresponse does cause A5's listening.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions failure and B4 reports silence, without establishing why B needs this expedition to succeed.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 changes the activity to listening, but adds little commitment beyond A3's knocking.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a map and wall, but no destination or practical reason to find the door.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'At doors' economically limits the admitted failure; A5 stays within the wall premise.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 65\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The treasure map ends at this solid wall. It secretly led us here to wake it. I'm knocking.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Nothing. And our torch is nearly out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then we were sent to hear it in darkness. I'm putting the torch out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can only hear you breathing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's it. The map was testing our breath control. I'm holding mine.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"Each failed operation reveals a supposedly concealed objective, which A immediately begins pursuing.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Optimistic reframing could celebrate failure without replacing the plan's intended goal.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 makes waking the wall the real mission, and A5 replaces that mission with listening through it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses physical exploration and dwindling torchlight; alternative b uses a radio operator's increasingly impractical assignments.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision gives B practical pressure and makes both failures redirect immediate action. Internal reread scores fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4: 90. B4 identifies breathing, causing A5 to declare and begin breath control.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised breath-control mission is a substantial irrational leap; sincerity must make it feel discovered rather than chosen for a joke.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D161-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ec379d4ea3184b83da1a059b582570c42a26de098a2dc9049032d0cff475bfa8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 recasts transmission as reception and A5 declares learning silence; A3 mostly salvages reception rather than changing goals.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's static becomes A3's rhythm transcription, and B4's stoppage becomes A5's recording of silence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 supply equipment updates but reveal no personal duty, urgency or competing choice.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes the mission more abstract but 'Keep recording' barely changes the practical commitment.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a failed signal, but the sender, recipient and purpose remain unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Write the rhythm' and A5's 'Keep recording' express the logic without explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 73\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Base can't hear our report. We were secretly sent to receive. I'm switching channels.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Still static. My shift ends in five minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then our real assignment is composing their anthem. I'm writing down the crackles.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The battery's dead. Your anthem has no ending.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"We were commissioned to perform it ourselves. I'll crackle; you take the bass.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats each failure as evidence of a different secret assignment and immediately performs that assignment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Persistence would keep trying to deliver the original report through another method.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 abandons sending, and A5 claims that learning silence was the purpose instead.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a moves explorers through bodily tasks; this version recruits a colleague who wants to finish a shift into a performance.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision preserves the radio setting while making the changing goals and B's stake concrete. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4: 90. B4 removes recorded sound, causing A5's live performance mission.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 starts a vocal assignment in dialogue rather than demonstrating a crackle; the immediate commitment is clear but less physical than alternative a.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D162-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"03c972f97d3df0f7ea70e8450926ab49cb173222ce8d647d802785737003104d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 invents elementary cloud anatomy to answer B2; A5 invents dampness as the locating rule only after B4 asks where.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's objection triggers A3's anatomical exception; B4's location question triggers A5's ruler test.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 make B a pupil asking the required questions, with no independent reason to measure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 provides a new rule but announces success without a new consequence or increased demand.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1's cloud measurement lacks an accessible setting; A5 assumes a ruler can become damp against the cloud.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'elementary cloud anatomy' efficiently performs authority; A5 keeps the joke tied to measurement.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before we sell this bottled cloud, measure from its third elbow.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm the new assistant. I can only find two.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Naturally. The third appears when addressed by its professional title. Say 'Professor.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Professor. It's raining inside the bottle.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. Those are its credentials. Count the drops; that gives us its sleeve size.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A projects complete expertise while supplying a fresh nonsensical rule precisely when a practical question requires one.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Random surreal description can name strange things without maintaining an expert's responsive authority.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 answers the elbow objection with anatomy, and A5 answers the location question with a dampness test.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This version makes A a shop expert inducting an assistant; alternative b makes A an expert responding to a malfunctioning gravity control.\",\n      \"reason\": \"A bottled-cloud shop is a new illustrative setting that makes measurement playable and gives B a role. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4: 90. B4's rain creates A5's credential and sizing rules.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final sleeve-size rule is deliberately strange; its connection to the established elbows needs confident, practical delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D162-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c0e1528712ec15dc7d2c4fa2bb363d5623639df6f4a93d4100e4a1e3df96f428\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 defines polite turning after B2 asks how; A5 interprets the newly reported click as acceptance.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 supplies B2's missing procedure, and A5 specifically interprets B4's click at six.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 requests instruction and B4 reports a result, but B has no visible purpose beyond assisting A's demonstration.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 confirms success without expanding the rule's demand or consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a playable dial but does not establish what the gravity adjustment is meant to accomplish.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's pauses and A5's accepting six maintain one compact logic of etiquette.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 76\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"To get your sofa off the ceiling, turn the gravity dial politely.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm losing my deposit. How do I turn politely?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Pause at each number. They dislike being passed over.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Six clicked, but now the armchairs are rising.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Six has invited witnesses. Apologize to the whole suite; private apologies don't count once furniture assembles.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A supplies authoritative rules of strange etiquette as each new operational problem makes them necessary.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Expanding an impossible world's lore would emphasize history and wider consequences rather than improvised procedural mastery.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 invents the turning procedure in response to B2, and A5 gives B4's click an expert interpretation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses supportive instruction of a novice; this version puts expertise under pressure from a tenant's deposit and multiplying floating furniture.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision adds a concrete repair objective and replaces mere confirmation with a consequential new rule. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4: 92. B4's rising chairs require A5's public apology.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final rule approaches broader worldbuilding, though its immediate use as a repair instruction keeps procedural mastery central.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D163-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7cf7d6c30ec6c312d751a205f23ce604caf7a2fcbe98d88211b748ed755baa29\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 extends a flying chair into a table and A5 adds a hovering waiter, each justified through hospitality.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's sufficiency objection; B4's acceptance of the table enables A5's service requirement.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 tries to limit the project and B4 concedes, but B's resources or purpose remain unclear.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands the accepted flying table into a staffed airborne meal.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes flying furniture but not whether these speakers are buying, building or arranging it.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's hospitality distinction earns A5's practical concern about landing for soup.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 75\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"For my restaurant, your flying chair needs a flying table. Dinner should travel with the diner.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can make the whole table circle the room.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Add a hovering waiter. Nobody should chase their soup.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The waiter can serve while flying upside down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then commission gravity-proof soup. I won't make customers rotate between courses.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A answers each impossible proposal by committing to a still more impossible provision with a practical justification.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic escalation can add extravagant objects without answering B's particular strange offer.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 adds a hovering waiter because B4 has accepted the flying table, preserving the hospitality rationale.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This version has a supplier actively offering greater capabilities; alternative b uses a host negotiating accommodation for an invisible guest.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision establishes a restaurant purchase and gives B two genuine strange offers to heighten. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 4, brevity 4: 93. B4's inverted service directly requires A5's soup invention.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The restaurant is a new illustrative setting. Gravity-proof soup is clear comic logic, but the actor must prioritize customer comfort over celebrating the invention.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D163-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0ebecf03498860a768c4c3d17c6ec04a232b04074aafea7aaeffb37e743826f7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 enlarges the invisible guest premise, but B2's headcount objection and B4's question offer no further strange capability.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2 by positing companions; A5 answers B4's quantity question by reserving the room.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 protects the invitation count and B4 seeks workable seating instructions.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 commits the entire room to the possible entourage introduced in A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a guest and places but leaves the hosts' event and authority vague.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 is compact, but untouched plates cannot clearly establish how many invisible diners attended.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 67\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your invisible guest is welcome at our wedding. I'll reserve a table for invisible companions.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She can bring an invisible orchestra as her gift.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll book an invisible ballroom too. Visible walls would spoil their entrance.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They can play tomorrow's music tonight.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then invite our future children. They shouldn't miss the first dance to their favorite song.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A commits to a greater impossibility in direct response to each strange offer, explaining its value to the occasion.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Inventing unseen extra guests alone could be assumption-making without reciprocal escalation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 justifies invisible companions through hospitality, but the lack of a new strange offer from B4 weakens the exact mechanism.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a escalates a commercial furnishing order; this version accepts wedding gifts with increasingly expansive family hospitality.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The wedding is a new illustrative setting retaining the invisible guest and companions. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 4, brevity 4: 93. B4 introduces tomorrow's music, which causes A5 to invite future listeners.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The last exchange broadens invisibility into time travel; B explicitly supplies that bridge, but the imaginative scope is larger than alternative a.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D164-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"614507c2a1cfe75c946d05cf136a1169b78b206a1dca082ad4c3764aa465caf8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 turns the cupboard error into cleanup convenience; A5 turns the attic error into stair-free blanket collection.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's garden destination makes A3's equipment useful, and B4's attic supplies A5's blankets.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants the garden and B4 keeps trying the door, though B's contribution remains mostly corrective.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 repeats the useful-supplies tactic without increasing commitment to the unreliable door.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's wrong cupboard and B2's garden establish an immediately understandable impossible route.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'without stairs' identifies a precise benefit of the attic mistake.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 84\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Our garden door opens into the broom cupboard. Wonderful, we can take a brush for the picnic crumbs.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Now it's the attic. I'm carrying all the sandwiches.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Perfect. Grab the picnic blanket without climbing stairs.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Now it's the cellar. The sandwiches are getting warm.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Built-in refrigeration. Put them beside the wine; this door is catering.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats each malfunction of an impossible door as an unexpectedly useful feature of that same system.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Failure Reveals the Mission would replace the picnic objective with a newly claimed secret purpose.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 and A5 keep preparing for the garden visit while praising benefits of the wrong destinations.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative makes domestic wrong turns useful for a picnic; alternative b exploits reversed time to improve a missed meeting.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original narrowly misses acceptance because its progression stays level. Internal reread of the revision: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4: 92. B4's cellar and warm sandwiches jointly cause A5 to entrust food storage to the malfunction.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised destinations still use a repeated discovery pattern; increased reliance on the door supplies the heightening.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D164-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"819abf11d8e9e2d5001518bd356daa4203e100cfd22bb0c8fac1a93b40133f4c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 present backward time as an advantage, but A5's paperwork benefit is difficult to specify.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's missed meeting; A5 does not explain how B4's missing invitation constitutes solved paperwork.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 correct the timeline without revealing a responsibility or desired next action.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 praises efficiency but adds no stronger use of the clock than A3's earlier arrival.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a backward clock and B2 a meeting, but the practical chronology remains hazy.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's 'early for yesterday' is concise; A5's paperwork claim sounds more like a tag than an earned benefit.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 60\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The office clock is dragging us backward. Excellent, we can attend the meeting we missed.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's gone too far. The invitation hasn't arrived.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Time to prepare before anyone expects us to.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Now the company doesn't exist. Who's paying us?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"We can negotiate our salaries before they set the budget. Keep the clock running.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts every damaging consequence of reversed time into another useful capability of the clock.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple denial would ignore the lost invitation or employer rather than identify benefits arising from those losses.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 uses the missed meeting to propose earlier arrival, preserving an advantage tied to the impossible system.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a finds practical household conveniences; this version grows from schedule recovery into opportunistic salary negotiation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision makes time reversal explicit and gives B a concrete employment concern. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 4, brevity 4: 93. B4's vanished company creates A5's pre-budget bargaining opportunity and commitment to continue.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The salary opportunity depends on comic access to the future founders; the scene implies that possibility without explaining it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D165-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"031bef62d2310e8020de8382c8076aa7235f24cdfa8758d33598c2c10d3af7a8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 accepts three heads while objecting to crumbs, but A5's concern follows genuine fire damage rather than an ordinary detail.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2 with napkin etiquette; A5's coaster does not clearly address B4's burning table.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 expresses disbelief and B4 reports danger, but neither turn establishes B's practical relationship to the guest.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 increases damage stakes, yet A remains similarly admonishing rather than increasingly alarmed about something ordinary.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's dragon, carpet and crumbs establish a clear domestic visit.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's three heads sharing one napkin is compact and follows A's household standards.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your dragon can stay for tea. Please keep the biscuit crumbs off my carpet.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"His three heads breathe fire. Should I take him outside?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"He's fine here. Wait, three mouths? I need three napkins immediately.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"He brought his own. They're paper.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Paper? At my good table? Fetch the linen before the neighbors see.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A calmly accepts the impossible guest while ordinary housekeeping and etiquette provoke increasing alarm.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General fussiness could complain equally about dragon fire and table manners, losing the contrast in emotional response.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 explicitly accepts the three heads and objects to crumbs; A5 blurs that contrast by responding to a burning table.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This version escalates social embarrassment during a guest visit; alternative b prioritizes protecting furniture during levitation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision separates calm acceptance of fire-breathing from alarm over ordinary napkins. Internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4: 92. B4's helpful paper napkins cause A5's stronger etiquette panic.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's concern is status-conscious as well as domestic; the growing alarm needs to remain sincere rather than knowingly absurd.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D165-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5350a3836ce22950c687280c0758f76a74cba1f2f6e9f64693dbc9ecf05563b4\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 dismisses floating furniture and prioritizes the wet cup; A5 still puts water damage before lowering the table.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 incorporates B2's rising table as travelling water damage; A5 conditions B4's proposed rescue on removing the cup.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers a practical intervention, and A5 materially changes its order.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 increases caution but gives little audible increase in alarm compared with A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's wet cup and floating table give actors a clear domestic problem.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'every altitude' attaches the impossible movement directly to the ordinary water ring.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 81\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The table can float while we pack. That wet cup needs a mat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's at the ceiling. I'll pull it down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Wait! You'll slide the cup across the polish. Get the stepladder.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll climb up. I've still got my muddy boots on.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Boots off! Socks too if they're damp. We are not adding footprints to that water ring.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats levitation as manageable while becoming more urgent about ordinary marks and moisture.\",\n      \"confusa